                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I had seen photographs, and none of them had mentioned the roof:
a ceiling so high it kept its own weather, steel ribs vanishing into haze,
my footsteps returning from somewhere I hadn’t walked yet. The brick
held the last daylight in its upper windows. Below it, somebody had
spent real money making the space smaller. Temporary walls, curtains,
a tent of soft gray panels, had carved the hall into manageable rooms:
reception, donor tables, a performance corner, and in the middle, a low
round platform ringed with chairs, like a fountain waiting for water.
   Somebody had laid white carpet over everything in between.
   It made the room look expensive and sound dead. My shoes made
no noise. The cavernous echo had been swallowed whole. Waiters
crossed ahead of us pushing racks of glasses, and not one wheel
squeaked.
   I stood there with my left hand on the trolley and heard the thing I’d
been afraid of for three months: nothing. No coughs, no phones, no
chair legs. Only the air handlers, far up in the ceiling.
   “Don’t,” said Manny, quietly, at my elbow.
   “I’m not.”
   “You’re doing the face.”
   “What face?”
   “The one where you leave.”
   He put his enormous hand on the back of my neck, once, like you
would with a horse, and took it away again. We went to find an outlet.



Vanessa found us beside a table of blank place cards, in a gray blazer,
her staff badge turned face-in against her ribs as if she hadn’t decided
whether to be proud of it.
   “Your equipment goes there,” she said, pointing toward the per‐
formance corner, a small stage with a screen behind it. “The green
room is behind it. That’s where they’ll want the object.”
   “The object stays with me.”
   “I know.” She looked at the carpet. “I’m telling you what they’re go‐
ing to say.”




